<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Winter solstice</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as hibernal solstice, midwinter</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The winter solstice is the moment when one of Earth's poles reaches its maximum tilt away from the Sun, giving that hemisphere its shortest day and longest night of the year. In the Northern Hemisphere it falls on 21 or 22 December and in the Southern on 20 or 21 June. Since prehistory it has been marked by festivals and rites in many cultures, as the point when daylight begins to increase again; monuments such as Newgrange in Ireland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stonehenge">Stonehenge</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> are aligned with its sunrise or sunset. In the ancient Roman calendar the solstice was dated to 25 December, which the emperor Aurelian made the festival of the birthday of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sol-invictus">Sol Invictus</a> in 274 CE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/winter-solstice.jpg" alt="Winter solstice" width="330" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vicky WJ from Brighton, UK · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stonehege_(2148722919)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The sun god's shrine was built so that light is focused upon it during the <b>winter solstice</b>.</blockquote><a href="/work/karnak">Karnak · What It Is</a></li><li><blockquote>…credited as the first scholar to study them in depth, flew over them in 1940 and 1941 while studying ancient irrigation systems, realised that one was in the shape of a bird, and saw how lines converged on the horizon at the <b>winter solstice</b> in the Southern Hemisphere.</blockquote><a href="/work/nazca-lines">Nazca Lines · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote>Peruvians continue to celebrate Inti Raymi, the annual Inca festival of the <b>winter solstice</b> and new year, which is held near Sacsayhuamán on 24 June.</blockquote><a href="/work/sacsayhuaman">Sacsayhuamán · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Roman Empire 25 December was the traditional date of the <b>winter solstice</b>.</blockquote><a href="/essays/christmas-day-in-history">Christmas Day in History</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>winter solstice</b> is the moment when one of Earth's poles reaches its greatest tilt away from the Sun, giving that hemisphere its shortest day and longest night of the year.</blockquote><a href="/essays/midwinter">Midwinter</a></li></ul></section>
